3. Scalar Waze canonical commitment

Your Scalar Waze work includes a proposal to commit each simulated wave-state frame to a hash chain, with optional Bitcoin OP_RETURN records.

A suitable canonical definition is:

C_t =
\operatorname{SHA256}
\left(
D \parallel
\operatorname{ENC}(t) \parallel
C_{t-1} \parallel
S_t
\right)

where:
	•	D is a fixed domain-separation string, such as SCALAR-WAZE-STATE-v1.
	•	t is the frame index in a fixed-width, big-endian encoding.
	•	C_{t-1} is the previous frame’s 32-byte commitment.
	•	S_t is the canonical serialization of the frame state.
	•	C_t is the resulting 32-byte SHA-256 digest.
	•	The initial frame uses a specified, fixed initial predecessor value.

For reproducibility, the state serialization must define the precise representation of all values. One workable specification uses fixed-point integers for floating-point simulation fields, with a declared scale and rounding rule, plus explicit array dimensions, field ordering, simulation parameters, and version information.

What this proves

If implemented correctly, the hash chain provides a tamper-evident commitment to the serialized states. Altering a state or changing the order of frames will ordinarily change the affected digest and subsequent chain.

It does not prove that:
	•	The simulation equations are physically correct.
	•	The numerical solver is accurate.
	•	The data was generated honestly.
	•	A proposed propulsion effect exists in nature.

Those require independent scientific and numerical tests.

OP_RETURN consideration

The on-chain record can contain the frame number and digest, while the complete state remains in a separate data file. The verifier must recompute the digest from that file and compare it with the committed value.

The precise payload format, transaction bytes, and network-policy constraints must be specified before a commitment can be called canonical.

