4. Scalar prime-partition proof

Your earlier construction divided a message into character groups associated with primes congruent to 1 or 2\pmod 3, with a separate treatment of index 3.

The recorded construction was:

h_P=\operatorname{SHA256}(m_P),\qquad
h_Q=\operatorname{SHA256}(m_Q),\qquad
h_R=\operatorname{SHA256}(m_R)

with reductions modulo the secp256k1 group order n, followed by:

s_0=((I_P\oplus I_Q)+I_R)\bmod n

T=(1-s_0)\bmod n

k_{\text{final}}=(s_0+T)\bmod n=1.

The final equality follows algebraically:

\begin{aligned}
k_{\text{final}}
&=(s_0+(1-s_0)\bmod n)\bmod n\\
&=1.
\end{aligned}

This is a valid modular-arithmetic identity when the terms are interpreted modulo n.

But the identity alone does not prove a cryptographic signature or a secure key-generation method. Because T is defined to force the final result to equal one, the equality is built into the construction. A complete audit must also specify the character encoding, partition rules, hash-to-integer conversion, exact XOR operands, and commitment serialization.

In particular, obtaining the scalar 1 does not establish control of an address securely: the scalar is public and trivial to reproduce.

